#!/usr/bin/env bash
# UserPromptSubmit hook for the drovr plugin — the per-turn gate.
#
# Injects a small card (<=600 bytes) before every user turn, re-asserting the
# routing rule the SessionStart reflex established: even a 1% chance a drovr:*
# skill applies means invoking it. A SessionStart injection is one prompt at the
# top of a session and scrolls out from under the agent as the context fills;
# the gate is what keeps the discipline reachable at turn 200.
#
# This hook is deliberately thin: it delegates every decision to
# `drovr reflex --gate`, which reads the `[reflex]` config (`enabled`,
# `per_turn`) and the transcript named on stdin, and prints the hook JSON or
# nothing at all. Keeping the logic in the CLI keeps it unit-testable.
#
# FOUR DELIBERATE DIFFERENCES FROM hooks/session-start. Every one is
# load-bearing; none is an oversight to be tidied away.
#
#   1. NO DROVR_PHASE SUPPRESSION. The SessionStart reflex no-ops inside a
#      drovr-spawned phase because the phase agent runs on its injected briefing.
#      The gate does the opposite: a phase is exactly where the discipline has to
#      hold, and a phase agent's briefing scrolls out of the window like anything
#      else. Adding an `if [ -n "${DROVR_PHASE:-}" ]` block here would silence
#      the gate precisely where it matters most — and the CLI does not check
#      DROVR_PHASE at all, so this script is the only place that asymmetry can be
#      got wrong.
#
#   2. STDIN IS PASSED THROUGH. Claude Code writes the hook payload — including
#      `transcript_path` — to stdin, and that path is the gate's only evidence
#      that the previous turn already invoked a drovr:* skill and needs no card.
#      drovr runs as a child and inherits this process's stdin unchanged.
#      Nothing in this script may consume stdin (no `read`, no heredoc): a
#      script that ate the payload would leave the gate emitting on every single
#      turn, which looks exactly like a healthy hook.
#
#   3. A MISSING BINARY DEGRADES SILENTLY, where session-start fails loudly.
#      See the `command -v` guard below.
#
#   4. IT NEVER EXITS 2. See the mapping at the bottom — on this harness exit 2
#      from a UserPromptSubmit hook discards the user's prompt.

set -euo pipefail

# NO PLUGIN-ROOT RESOLUTION, deliberately. hooks/session-start resolves
# CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT because it must locate skills/using-drovr/SKILL.md on disk.
# The gate card is a `const` compiled into the CLI, which reads
# CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT nowhere at all — so the same block here would be dead code
# that still fails: `PLUGIN_ROOT="$(cd "${SCRIPT_DIR}/.." && pwd)"` under
# `set -e` aborts the hook if that directory is unreadable or the script is
# reached through a broken symlink, silencing the gate for a reason that has
# nothing to do with the gate. That is fail-CLOSED inside a fail-open design.
# If a future card ever reads from disk, resolve the root then.
# Pinned by `user_prompt_hook_needs_no_plugin_root`.

DROVR="${DROVR_BIN:-drovr}"

# NO drovr INSTALLED -> DEGRADE SILENTLY. The plugin is installable on its own
# (`.claude-plugin/marketplace.json`) while the CLI is a separate build-and-PATH
# step, so "plugin without binary" is a normal state, not a broken one. There is
# no drovr discipline to re-assert when drovr is not installed, and a hook that
# complained here would complain before EVERY PROMPT OF EVERY SESSION, forever.
# hooks/session-start still fails loudly in that case — once per session, which
# is the right number of times to tell someone their CLI is missing.
#
# `command -v` is a resolvability check, NOT an executability check: for a bare
# name found on PATH it returns 0 even when the file is mode 644. That case
# falls through to bash's own 126, which the mapping below turns into 1 — safe,
# just not silent.
#
# NOTE THE OTHER HALF OF THIS TRADE, which the argument above does not cover: a
# `drovr` that IS installed but predates `--gate` — the more likely of the two
# states — exits non-zero with clap's usage error on stderr, before every
# prompt, forever. That noise is deliberate. It is recoverable by rebuilding;
# an eaten prompt is not, and silence would hide a gate that had stopped
# working. Loud-and-working beats quiet-and-broken; only "not installed at all"
# is quiet, because there is no discipline to re-assert in that state.
command -v "$DROVR" >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0

# NEVER `exec`, and NEVER let a non-zero status through unmapped.
#
# MEASURED on Claude Code 2.1.220: a `UserPromptSubmit` hook that exits **2** is
# a BLOCKING error — the user's prompt is discarded unprocessed and the harness
# prints "Original prompt: …" instead of answering it. Other non-zero codes are
# non-blocking (127 was verified to let the turn through untouched).
#
# `exec` would hand the child's status straight to the harness, and clap exits
# **2** on a usage error. The binary is installed by hand, independently of the
# plugin's hooks/, so a `drovr` predating `--gate` is the expected skew — and it
# would erase every prompt the user typed, in every session, with only stderr as
# a clue. Mapping every failure reached here to 1 keeps it loud and visible while
# making it structurally impossible for this hook to eat a prompt. (The one
# non-zero path that does NOT reach here is the `command -v` guard above, which
# exits 0 on purpose.)
#
# stdin still reaches drovr: a child inherits the shell's stdin, so dropping
# `exec` costs a fork, not the payload. `set -e` does not fire on the left of
# `||`, which is why the mapping is written this way rather than as an `if`.
"$DROVR" reflex --gate || exit 1
